# Date & Time Picker

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A date or time picker lets people give a day, a time, or a range of days. There are two families. The browser draws its own field for `<input type="date">` and its relatives. Home Assistant draws its own date, time and date-range pickers, which you meet in automations, helpers, History and Energy. A dashboard author cannot add either family to a card, but can ask for a known date with three plain fields in a custom panel or form.

This page owns the picker elements and the three-field date pattern. For buttons and form behaviour, see [Buttons and forms](controls.html).

This page covers:

- Date & Time Picker
  - `<input type="date">`
  - `<input type="time">`
  - `<input type="datetime-local">`
  - `<input type="month">`
  - `<input type="week">`
  - `ha-date-input`
  - `ha-time-input`
  - `ha-date-range-picker`
  - `ha-dialog-date-picker`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- **Browser fields:** one field with parts for the day, month and year, or hours and minutes, and often a calendar or clock button. The browser decides the look.
- **Home Assistant date field:** a read-only field with a calendar icon at the end. Pressing it opens a calendar.
- **Home Assistant time field:** boxes for hours and minutes, and seconds when asked, with an am and pm choice on a 12-hour clock.
- **Date range picker:** a field that opens a calendar with ready-made ranges. On a small screen it opens as a sheet.
- **Three-field date:** a question, a format hint and three labelled boxes for day, month and year.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

All five browser fields share the attributes `min`, `max`, `step`, `required`, `readonly` and `disabled`. Give each a visible label. A placeholder is not a label. The look, the keyboard use and the language of the picker differ between browsers, and support for the month and week fields is not the same everywhere. Test them in the browsers your household uses.

What the Civica theme sets for Home Assistant’s own form fields, which include the date and time fields: a 2 pixel border in the text colour, square corners and a white field in light mode or the dark surface in dark mode. The focus colour is the Civica yellow. The calendar’s selected-day colour and layout are Home Assistant’s own.

<h3 id="child-input-type-date"><code>&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot;&gt;</code></h3>

Chooses a day. Its value is written `YYYY-MM-DD`. It carries no time zone.

<h3 id="child-input-type-time"><code>&lt;input type=&quot;time&quot;&gt;</code></h3>

Chooses a time of day. Its value is written `HH:MM`, or `HH:MM:SS` when seconds are used.

<h3 id="child-input-type-datetime-local"><code>&lt;input type=&quot;datetime-local&quot;&gt;</code></h3>

Chooses a day and a time together. Its value is written `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM` and has no time zone, so say which time zone you mean next to the field.

<h3 id="child-input-type-month"><code>&lt;input type=&quot;month&quot;&gt;</code></h3>

Chooses a month and year. Its value is written `YYYY-MM`.

<h3 id="child-input-type-week"><code>&lt;input type=&quot;week&quot;&gt;</code></h3>

Chooses a week of a year. Its value is written `YYYY-Www`. Most households do not think in week numbers, so prefer a date range.

<h3 id="child-ha-date-input"><code>ha-date-input</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s date field. It shows the chosen date in the person’s own date format, with a calendar icon.

- It is read-only. People cannot type a date into it; pressing it, or pressing Space or Enter, opens the calendar. Where the field can be cleared, Backspace or Delete clears it.
- It has a label, an accessible label, helper text, `min`, `max`, `disabled` and `required`. The earliest date it allows by default is 1 January 1970.
- It holds a date only, with no time. It tells the page when the value changes.
- In an automation or helper, the `date` selector uses it, and the `datetime` selector uses it beside the time field.

<h3 id="child-ha-time-input"><code>ha-time-input</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s time field. It shows boxes for hours and minutes, and for seconds when `enable-second` is on. The clock is 12-hour or 24-hour to suit the person’s language and region. It has a label, helper text, `disabled` and `required`, and tells the page when the value changes. The `time` selector uses it.

<h3 id="child-ha-date-range-picker"><code>ha-date-range-picker</code></h3>

Chooses a start and an end date, with ready-made ranges and an optional time. On a wide screen it opens as a popover. On a small screen it opens as a bottom sheet (see [Action Bar / Bottom Sheet](action-bar-bottom-sheet.html)). It has previous and next buttons to move between months. Home Assistant uses it for the History and Energy date ranges. Keep these native controls for exploring dates. Do not replace them with three fields.

<h3 id="child-ha-dialog-date-picker"><code>ha-dialog-date-picker</code></h3>

The calendar that `ha-date-input` opens. It shows a month, with previous and next month buttons, a Cancel and a Set button, and can limit the choice between a minimum and a maximum date. It also offers a clear button where the field allows clearing. A dashboard author cannot open it directly.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A calendar suits finding a day in the near future or past and checking the weekday.
- A calendar is slow for a date the person already knows, such as an installation date years ago. Three fields are quicker and easier to check.
- `ha-date-input` does not accept typing, so a person who cannot use a calendar comfortably has no other route in that field.
- Browser date fields differ in look and behaviour, and the calendar cannot be restyled from a theme.
- A date and time with no time zone can be read in the wrong zone. State the zone.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use Home Assistant’s own pickers where Home Assistant shows them. Do not rebuild them. For a date that people know, use three fields. For a date range on a chart, keep Home Assistant’s range picker, which opens as the sheet described on [Action Bar / Bottom Sheet](action-bar-bottom-sheet.html).

### Ask for a known date with three fields

Use day, month and year fields for something like the date a filter was replaced or a device was installed. Keep the units visible and accept a leading zero without making people retype.

- Ask one question and group the fields in a fieldset, with the question as its legend. Give each part its own label and a numeric keyboard on a phone. Show the expected format, such as “For example, 11 11 2026”, as a hint and link it to the group.
- Validate the whole date, including 31 February and leap years. Say which part is wrong, for example “Month must be between 1 and 12”, and keep the parts that are correct. Link an error summary to the first wrong part.
- A date-only record is not a timestamp. Do not shift it when the time zone changes.
- Do not use birthday autofill hints for appliance dates.
- Explain a limit in plain words, for example “The date cannot be in the future”, rather than calling a date “invalid”.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: a visible label and a format hint.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: name the wrong part and say how to fix it.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: keep the valid parts after an error.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the calendar and its buttons work by keyboard and are large enough to press.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: every part has a name.

Test missing parts, 31 February, an unexpected future date and a phone number keyboard. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

